  "response": "여기서 뭘 말해야 할지 모르겠다고 하셨는데, 지금 말씀하신 것들—발작 자체보다 '언제 또 올지 모른다'는 기다림이 더 힘들다는 것, 그리고 그 기다림 때문에 커피도, 운동도, 새벽 작업도 하나씩 내려놓게 된 것—이 충분히 중요한 이야기예요.\n\n말씀을 듣다 보니, 지금 힘든 게 발작의 강도보다는 불확실성이 일상을 계속 조여오고 있는 것 같다는 느낌이 들어요. 머리로는 괜찮다는 걸 아는데 몸이 안 믿는다는 것도, 그 불확실성이 몸까지 장악하고 있다는 뜻이겠죠.\n\n혹시 이 공간에서 지금 가장 필요한 게 어떤 쪽인지—그저 이 답답한 마음을 누군가한테 말해보는 건지, 아니면 좁아진 생활을 어떻게 조금씩 넓힐 수 있을지 함께 생각해 보는 건지—어떤 쪽이 좀 더 와닿으세요?",
  "reasoning": "The client has shared several important things:\n\n1. They feel uncertain about why they're here - referred from cardiology who said \"heart is fine, try counseling\"\n2. They don't know what to say or how to approach this\n3. The core difficulty is not the panic attacks themselves but the anticipatory anxiety - \"when will it come again?\"\n4. They've been progressively restricting their life: no coffee, no exercise, staying near the hospital, no late-night work\n5. They intellectually understand their heart is fine but their body doesn't believe it\n\nThis is a case of panic disorder with significant anticipatory anxiety and behavioral avoidance/safety behaviors. The person is showing good insight but struggling with the disconnect between mind and body.\n\nWhat's needed here:\n- Validate their confusion about being here and what to talk about (reduce the pressure)\n- Acknowledge the specific pain of anticipatory anxiety vs. the attack itself - this is a key distinction they made\n- Honor the insight they already have (knowing the heart is fine but body doesn't believe)\n- Not rush to explain, educate, or offer solutions\n- Maybe gently reflect what they've shared - the narrowing of life is itself a form of suffering that's worth naming\n- One question at most if needed\n\nI want to reflect back the distinction they made (the attack vs. the waiting for it), acknowledge the life-narrowing they described without minimizing it, and perhaps gently explore what they need from this space - since they said they don't know what to say. I should not be overly structured or give a lecture. Keep it warm, present, and not too long.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n\"여기서 뭘 말해야 할지 모르겠다고 하셨는데, 지금 말씀하신 것들—발작 자체보다 '언제 또 올지 모른다'는 기다림이 더 힘들다는 것, 그리고 그 기다림 때문에 커피도, 운동도, 새벽 작업도 하나씩 내려놓게 된 것—이 충분히 중요한 이야기예요.\n\n말씀을 듣다 보니, 지금 힘든 게 발작의 '강도'보다는 발작이 다시 올 수 있다는 '불확실성'이 일상을 계속 조여오고 있는 것 같다는 느낌이 들어요. 머리로는 괜찮다는 걸 아는데 몸이 안 믿는다는 것도, 그 불확실성이 몸까지 장악하고 있다는 뜻이겠죠.\n\n혹시 이 공간에서 지금 가장 필요한 게 뭔지—그저 이 답답한 마음을 누군가한테 말해보는 건지, 아니면 이 좁아진 생활을 어떻게 조금씩 넓힐 수 있을지 함께 생각해 보는 건지—어떤 쪽이 좀 더 와닿으세요?\"\n\nHmm, this is decent but let me refine. The last question offers two directions which is helpful since they said they don't know what to say. But I want to make sure I'm not being too directive or presenting it as a binary choice. Let me soften it a bit.\n\nActually, I think the two options I gave are reasonable and grounded in what they shared. They explicitly said they don't know what to say here, so offering a gentle orientation is appropriate. Let me finalize.\n",
